  ["Linux", "What is the difference between a bridge, a bond, and a team in Linux networking?", "bridge-bond-team", "Compare network interface types.", "**Direct answer :**\nA bridge is a virtual switch that connects multiple network interfaces at Layer 2. A bond (bonding) combines multiple physical interfaces for redundancy or performance (mode 0-6). Team is a newer alternative to bonding with better features and management. A bridge behaves like a software Layer-2 switch, while bonding combines interfaces into one logical link for redundancy or throughput. Linux bonding is established and widely supported; the historical `team` approach is not universally preferred on modern distributions, so the platform's supported networking stack matters.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand What is the difference between a bridge, a bond, and a team in Linux networking and can explain it clearly in a practical engineering context. They are looking for correct terminology, sound reasoning, and awareness of important trade-offs or limitations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n- Start with the core definition or principle.\n- Explain how it works and why it matters in practice.\n- Connect it to the scenario described in the question.\n- Mention one important trade-off, limitation, or common misconception.\n- If relevant, give a small example rather than only listing terminology.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with a one-sentence direct answer to the question. Then explain the mechanism or reasoning in two or three points, using the terminology from the question. Add a short real-world example or trade-off, and finish by stating when you would use the approach or what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nLikely follow-up questions include: “What is Network Namespace and how is it used?”; “How does VXLAN work in Linux?”. Be ready to compare alternatives, discuss edge cases, and explain how you would validate the result."] as const,
  ["Linux", "What is Network Namespace and how is it used?", "network-namespace", "Explain network isolation.", "**Direct answer :**\nA network namespace provides a completely isolated network stack (interfaces, routes, firewall rules). It is used in containers to provide each container with its own network environment. Use `ip netns` to manage network namespaces. Each network namespace has its own interfaces, routes, firewall state, and socket namespace. A common container setup connects a namespace to the host through a virtual Ethernet pair and a bridge or other network device.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand What is Network Namespace and how is it used and can explain it clearly in a practical engineering context. They are looking for correct terminology, sound reasoning, and awareness of important trade-offs or limitations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n- Start with the core definition or principle.\n- Explain how it works and why it matters in practice.\n- Connect it to the scenario described in the question.\n- Mention one important trade-off, limitation, or common misconception.\n- If relevant, give a small example rather than only listing terminology.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with a one-sentence direct answer to the question. Then explain the mechanism or reasoning in two or three points, using the terminology from the question. Add a short real-world example or trade-off, and finish by stating when you would use the approach or what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nLikely follow-up questions include: “How does VXLAN work in Linux?”; “What is the purpose of the `tc` (traffic control) command?”. Be ready to compare alternatives, discuss edge cases, and explain how you would validate the result."] as const,
  ["Linux", "How does VXLAN work in Linux?", "vxlan", "Explain virtual network encapsulation.", "**Direct answer :**\nVXLAN (Virtual Extensible LAN) is a network virtualization technology that encapsulates Layer 2 frames in UDP packets. It allows creating overlay networks across multiple hosts, overcoming the limitations of VLANs (4096 IDs). Linux supports VXLAN via the kernel module. VXLAN encapsulates an inner Ethernet frame in UDP, commonly using UDP port 4789. A 24-bit VNI provides a much larger logical network identifier space than traditional 12-bit VLAN IDs, making it useful for overlays across routed underlays.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand How does VXLAN work in Linux and can explain it clearly in a practical engineering context. They are looking for correct terminology, sound reasoning, and awareness of important trade-offs or limitations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n- Start with the core definition or principle.\n- Explain how it works and why it matters in practice.\n- Connect it to the scenario described in the question.\n- Mention one important trade-off, limitation, or common misconception.\n- If relevant, give a small example rather than only listing terminology.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with a one-sentence direct answer to the question. Then explain the mechanism or reasoning in two or three points, using the terminology from the question. Add a short real-world example or trade-off, and finish by stating when you would use the approach or what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nLikely follow-up questions include: “What is the purpose of the `tc` (traffic control) command?”; “What is the difference between full, incremental, and differential backups?”. Be ready to compare alternatives, discuss edge cases, and explain how you would validate the result."] as const,
  ["Linux", "What is the purpose of the `tc` (traffic control) command?", "tc", "Explain network traffic shaping.", "**Direct answer :**\n`tc` is used for traffic control in Linux, allowing you to configure QoS (Quality of Service), bandwidth limits, and packet scheduling. It works with disciplines (qdiscs), classes, and filters. Traffic control operates through qdiscs, classes, filters, and actions. It can shape or schedule egress traffic and, with suitable mechanisms, influence ingress processing. Good designs measure actual bottlenecks before adding complex QoS rules.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand What is the purpose of the `tc` (traffic control) command and can explain it clearly in a practical engineering context. They are looking for correct terminology, sound reasoning, and awareness of important trade-offs or limitations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n- Start with the core definition or principle.\n- Explain how it works and why it matters in practice.\n- Connect it to the scenario described in the question.\n- Mention one important trade-off, limitation, or common misconception.\n- If relevant, give a small example rather than only listing terminology.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with a one-sentence direct answer to the question. Then explain the mechanism or reasoning in two or three points, using the terminology from the question. Add a short real-world example or trade-off, and finish by stating when you would use the approach or what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nLikely follow-up questions include: “What is the difference between full, incremental, and differential backups?”; “How do you perform a backup using `rsync`?”. Be ready to compare alternatives, discuss edge cases, and explain how you would validate the result."] as const,
  ["Linux", "What is the difference between full, incremental, and differential backups?", "backup-types", "Explain backup strategies.", "**Direct answer :**\nFull backup: copies all data. Incremental backup: copies only changes since the last backup (full or incremental). Differential backup: copies changes since the last full backup. Incremental backups save space but are slower to restore; differential backups are faster to restore but use more space. The restore chain matters as much as backup volume: a full backup can stand alone, while an incremental chain may require the full backup plus every required incremental. Differential backups require the full backup plus the latest differential. Backups should also be tested through actual restores.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand What is the difference between full, incremental, and differential backups and can explain it clearly in a practical engineering context. They are looking for correct terminology, sound reasoning, and awareness of important trade-offs or limitations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n- Start with the core definition or principle.\n- Explain how it works and why it matters in practice.\n- Connect it to the scenario described in the question.\n- Mention one important trade-off, limitation, or common misconception.\n- If relevant, give a small example rather than only listing terminology.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with a one-sentence direct answer to the question. Then explain the mechanism or reasoning in two or three points, using the terminology from the question. Add a short real-world example or trade-off, and finish by stating when you would use the approach or what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nLikely follow-up questions include: “How do you perform a backup using `rsync`?”; “What is the purpose of the `tar` command?”. Be ready to compare alternatives, discuss edge cases, and explain how you would validate the result."] as const,
  ["Linux", "How do you perform a backup using `rsync`?", "rsync-backup", "Explain rsync usage.", "**Direct answer :**\n`rsync -avz /source/ /destination/` synchronizes files and directories. `-a` preserves attributes, `-v` is verbose, `-z` compresses. For remote backups: `rsync -avz /source/ user@host:/destination/`. `rsync` is efficient because it copies only changed blocks. `rsync` compares file metadata and, when needed, uses its delta-transfer algorithm to reduce network transfer for changed files. `-a` means archive mode, while `-z` compresses data during transfer; compression is not always beneficial on fast networks or already-compressed data.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand How do you perform a backup using `rsync` and can explain it clearly in a practical engineering context. They are looking for correct terminology, sound reasoning, and awareness of important trade-offs or limitations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n- Start with the core definition or principle.\n- Explain how it works and why it matters in practice.\n- Connect it to the scenario described in the question.\n- Mention one important trade-off, limitation, or common misconception.\n- If relevant, give a small example rather than only listing terminology.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with a one-sentence direct answer to the question. Then explain the mechanism or reasoning in two or three points, using the terminology from the question. Add a short real-world example or trade-off, and finish by stating when you would use the approach or what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nLikely follow-up questions include: “What is the purpose of the `tar` command?”; “How do you restore a file from a backup?”. Be ready to compare alternatives, discuss edge cases, and explain how you would validate the result."] as const,
  ["Linux", "What is the purpose of the `tar` command?", "tar", "Explain archiving.", "**Direct answer :**\n`tar` (Tape Archive) is used to create archives (collections of files) and compress them. Common uses: `tar -czvf archive.tar.gz /path` creates a compressed archive, `tar -xzvf archive.tar.gz` extracts it. `tar` creates archives; compression is provided by an external compressor or tar's compression options, such as gzip with `-z`. An archive is not inherently a backup system because it does not by itself provide versioning, off-site retention, integrity monitoring, or tested restore workflows.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand What is the purpose of the `tar` command and can explain it clearly in a practical engineering context. They are looking for correct terminology, sound reasoning, and awareness of important trade-offs or limitations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n- Start with the core definition or principle.\n- Explain how it works and why it matters in practice.\n- Connect it to the scenario described in the question.\n- Mention one important trade-off, limitation, or common misconception.\n- If relevant, give a small example rather than only listing terminology.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with a one-sentence direct answer to the question. Then explain the mechanism or reasoning in two or three points, using the terminology from the question. Add a short real-world example or trade-off, and finish by stating when you would use the approach or what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nLikely follow-up questions include: “How do you restore a file from a backup?”; “What is the principle of least privilege and how is it applied in Linux?”. Be ready to compare alternatives, discuss edge cases, and explain how you would validate the result."] as const,
  ["Linux", "How do you restore a file from a backup?", "restore-backup", "Explain recovery steps.", "**Direct answer :**\nIf using `rsync`, copy the file back from the backup location. If using `tar`, extract the specific file: `tar -xvf backup.tar --wildcards 'path/to/file'`. If using a versioned backup system (e.g., `restic`), use the restore command. Recovery should verify the backup version, destination, permissions, ownership, and integrity before replacing production data. For databases and other stateful systems, application-consistent backups are preferable to blindly restoring filesystem files.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand How do you restore a file from a backup and can explain it clearly in a practical engineering context. They are looking for correct terminology, sound reasoning, and awareness of important trade-offs or limitations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n- Start with the core definition or principle.\n- Explain how it works and why it matters in practice.\n- Connect it to the scenario described in the question.\n- Mention one important trade-off, limitation, or common misconception.\n- If relevant, give a small example rather than only listing terminology.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with a one-sentence direct answer to the question. Then explain the mechanism or reasoning in two or three points, using the terminology from the question. Add a short real-world example or trade-off, and finish by stating when you would use the approach or what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nLikely follow-up questions include: “What is the principle of least privilege and how is it applied in Linux?”; “What is AppArmor and how does it compare to SELinux?”. Be ready to compare alternatives, discuss edge cases, and explain how you would validate the result."] as const,
  ["Linux", "What is the principle of least privilege and how is it applied in Linux?", "least-privilege", "Explain security best practices.", "**Direct answer :**\nThe principle of least privilege states that users and processes should have only the minimum permissions necessary to perform their functions. In Linux, this is enforced through file permissions, sudo policies, and SELinux/AppArmor. Least privilege applies to both humans and services: minimize Unix permissions, sudo commands, Linux capabilities, service accounts, filesystem access, and network reachability. Periodically review privileges because old access often persists after responsibilities change.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand What is the principle of least privilege and how is it applied in Linux and can explain it clearly in a practical engineering context. They are looking for correct terminology, sound reasoning, and awareness of important trade-offs or limitations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n- Start with the core definition or principle.\n- Explain how it works and why it matters in practice.\n- Connect it to the scenario described in the question.\n- Mention one important trade-off, limitation, or common misconception.\n- If relevant, give a small example rather than only listing terminology.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with a one-sentence direct answer to the question. Then explain the mechanism or reasoning in two or three points, using the terminology from the question. Add a short real-world example or trade-off, and finish by stating when you would use the approach or what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nLikely follow-up questions include: “What is AppArmor and how does it compare to SELinux?”; “What is a security audit in Linux and how do you perform one?”. Be ready to compare alternatives, discuss edge cases, and explain how you would validate the result."] as const,
  ["Linux", "What is AppArmor and how does it compare to SELinux?", "apparmor-vs-selinux", "Compare MAC systems.", "**Direct answer :**\nAppArmor is a Mandatory Access Control (MAC) system that restricts applications based on pathnames. It is simpler to configure than SELinux and is the default on Ubuntu. SELinux uses more complex labeled security policies and is the default on RHEL/CentOS. AppArmor primarily expresses policy through profiles associated with application paths, while SELinux uses security labels and policy rules. Both provide mandatory access control; neither should be judged solely by which is 'simpler' because operational tooling and organizational expertise matter.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand What is AppArmor and how does it compare to SELinux and can explain it clearly in a practical engineering context. They are looking for correct terminology, sound reasoning, and awareness of important trade-offs or limitations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n- Start with the core definition or principle.\n- Explain how it works and why it matters in practice.\n- Connect it to the scenario described in the question.\n- Mention one important trade-off, limitation, or common misconception.\n- If relevant, give a small example rather than only listing terminology.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with a one-sentence direct answer to the question. Then explain the mechanism or reasoning in two or three points, using the terminology from the question. Add a short real-world example or trade-off, and finish by stating when you would use the approach or what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nLikely follow-up questions include: “What is a security audit in Linux and how do you perform one?”; “What is the purpose of the `/etc/sudoers` file and how do you edit it safely?”. Be ready to compare alternatives, discuss edge cases, and explain how you would validate the result."] as c