  ["Android", "How do you handle network timeouts?", "network-timeouts", "Explain timeout handling.", "**Direct answer :**\nSet timeouts in OkHttp: `connectTimeout`, `readTimeout`, `writeTimeout`. Use `Retrofit.Builder().client()` to set. Also handle `SocketTimeoutException`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand How do you handle network timeouts and can explain it clearly in a practical engineering context. They are looking for correct terminology, sound reasoning, and awareness of important trade-offs or limitations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n- Start with the core definition or principle.\n- Explain how it works and why it matters in practice.\n- Connect it to the scenario described in the question.\n- Mention one important trade-off, limitation, or common misconception.\n- If relevant, give a small example rather than only listing terminology.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with a one-sentence direct answer to the question. Then explain the mechanism or reasoning in two or three points, using the terminology from the question. Add a short real-world example or trade-off, and finish by stating when you would use the approach or what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nLikely follow-up questions include: “How do you cache network responses?”; “How do you handle authentication with Retrofit?”. Be ready to compare alternatives, discuss edge cases, and explain how you would validate the result."] as const,
  ["Android", "How do you cache network responses?", "network-caching", "Explain caching.", "**Direct answer :**\nUse OkHttp's built-in cache with a `Cache` object. Set cache-control headers on the server. On the client, use `Cache-Control` to override.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand How do you cache network responses and can explain it clearly in a practical engineering context. They are looking for correct terminology, sound reasoning, and awareness of important trade-offs or limitations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n- Start with the core definition or principle.\n- Explain how it works and why it matters in practice.\n- Connect it to the scenario described in the question.\n- Mention one important trade-off, limitation, or common misconception.\n- If relevant, give a small example rather than only listing terminology.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with a one-sentence direct answer to the question. Then explain the mechanism or reasoning in two or three points, using the terminology from the question. Add a short real-world example or trade-off, and finish by stating when you would use the approach or what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nLikely follow-up questions include: “How do you handle authentication with Retrofit?”; “What is the `NetworkOnMainThreadException`?”. Be ready to compare alternatives, discuss edge cases, and explain how you would validate the result."] as const,
  ["Android", "How do you handle authentication with Retrofit?", "retrofit-auth", "Explain authentication.", "**Direct answer :**\nUse an Interceptor to add headers (e.g., `Authorization: Bearer token`). Refresh tokens with a custom `Authenticator` that retries after token refresh.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand How do you handle authentication with Retrofit and can explain it clearly in a practical engineering context. They are looking for correct terminology, sound reasoning, and awareness of important trade-offs or limitations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n- Start with the core definition or principle.\n- Explain how it works and why it matters in practice.\n- Connect it to the scenario described in the question.\n- Mention one important trade-off, limitation, or common misconception.\n- If relevant, give a small example rather than only listing terminology.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with a one-sentence direct answer to the question. Then explain the mechanism or reasoning in two or three points, using the terminology from the question. Add a short real-world example or trade-off, and finish by stating when you would use the approach or what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nLikely follow-up questions include: “What is the `NetworkOnMainThreadException`?”; “What is the difference between `http://` and `https://`?”. Be ready to compare alternatives, discuss edge cases, and explain how you would validate the result."] as const,
  ["Android", "What is the `NetworkOnMainThreadException`?", "network-on-main", "Explain exception.", "**Direct answer :**\nNetworkOnMainThreadException can occur when an application targeting Honeycomb/API 11 or later performs a network operation on the main thread. The UI thread should remain responsive, so network I/O belongs in a coroutine, executor, or another appropriate background mechanism. In modern Kotlin code, a common pattern is a suspend API call invoked from a lifecycle-aware scope and moved to Dispatchers.IO when the underlying operation is blocking. AsyncTask is deprecated and should not be recommended for new code.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand What is the `NetworkOnMainThreadException` and can explain it clearly in a practical engineering context. They are looking for correct terminology, sound reasoning, and awareness of important trade-offs or limitations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n- Start with the core definition or principle.\n- Explain how it works and why it matters in practice.\n- Connect it to the scenario described in the question.\n- Mention one important trade-off, limitation, or common misconception.\n- If relevant, give a small example rather than only listing terminology.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with a one-sentence direct answer to the question. Then explain the mechanism or reasoning in two or three points, using the terminology from the question. Add a short real-world example or trade-off, and finish by stating when you would use the approach or what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nLikely follow-up questions include: “What is the difference between `http://` and `https://`?”; “What are the ways to perform background tasks in Android?”. Be ready to compare alternatives, discuss edge cases, and explain how you would validate the result."] as const,
  ["Android", "What is the difference between `http://` and `https://`?", "http-vs-https", "Compare protocols.", "**Direct answer :**\nHTTP sends requests without transport encryption, while HTTPS uses TLS to provide confidentiality and server authentication and to protect data against tampering in transit. Android 9/API 28 and higher disable cleartext traffic by default for apps targeting API 28+, although an app can explicitly opt in through its network security configuration. Production applications should use HTTPS for sensitive and ordinary API traffic and should not disable certificate validation.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand What is the difference between `http://` and `https://` and can explain it clearly in a practical engineering context. They are looking for correct terminology, sound reasoning, and awareness of important trade-offs or limitations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n- Start with the core definition or principle.\n- Explain how it works and why it matters in practice.\n- Connect it to the scenario described in the question.\n- Mention one important trade-off, limitation, or common misconception.\n- If relevant, give a small example rather than only listing terminology.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with a one-sentence direct answer to the question. Then explain the mechanism or reasoning in two or three points, using the terminology from the question. Add a short real-world example or trade-off, and finish by stating when you would use the approach or what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nLikely follow-up questions include: “What are the ways to perform background tasks in Android?”; “What is AsyncTask and why is it deprecated?”. Be ready to compare alternatives, discuss edge cases, and explain how you would validate the result."] as const,
  ["Android", "What are the ways to perform background tasks in Android?", "background-tasks", "List background task options.", "**Direct answer :**\nChoose the background mechanism based on the job's lifetime and requirements. Coroutines and executors are appropriate for work tied to a currently running component. WorkManager is intended for deferrable work that should continue reliably even if the app process exits, with constraints and retry support. A foreground service is for ongoing user-visible work that qualifies under Android's foreground-service rules. Handler/Looper can schedule work on a thread's message queue. AsyncTask and JobIntentService are deprecated and should not be recommended for new code.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nInterviewers ask this to check whether you understand What are the ways to perform background tasks in Android and can explain it clearly in a practical engineering context. They are looking for correct terminology, sound reasoning, and awareness of important trade-offs or limitations.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n- Start with the core definition or principle.\n- Explain how it works and why it matters in practice.\n- Connect it to the scenario described in the question.\n- Mention one important trade-off, limitation, or common misconception.\n- If relevant, give a small example rather than only listing terminology.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with a one-sentence direct answer to the question. Then explain the mechanism or reasoning in two or three points, using the terminology from the question. Add a short real-world example or trade-off, and finish by stating when you would use the approach or what you would verify.\n\n**Follow-up angle to anticipate :**\nLikely follow-up questions include: “What is AsyncTask and why is it deprecated?”; “What is the main thread and why should you avoid blocking it?”. Be ready to compare alternatives, discuss edge cases, and explain how you would validate the result."] as co